# Line endings, normalised session 23.
#
# WHY: this repo ran with core.autocrlf=true and no attributes file, which stores LF in the
# index but hands you CRLF in the working tree. Three costs, all of them real here:
#
#   1. A one-line change once showed up as a 261-line rewrite in a diff.
#   2. reference/prompt_archive/MANIFEST.json records md5_lf -- hashes over LF bytes -- so a
#      fresh checkout's actual files did NOT match their own manifest. That file's line_endings
#      note had to warn about it. With eol=lf they match, and the warning is now just true.
#   3. Mixed endings broke exact-string edits against CRLF files (scripts/*.py) while the
#      identical edit worked against LF files (docs/*.md). 218 of ~400 tracked files were CRLF.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` pins the working-tree ending for everything
# it decides IS text. An explicit eol= overrides core.autocrlf, so that setting no longer
# matters and does not need changing.
#
# No binary carve-outs are listed because at the time of writing NOTHING binary is tracked --
# the repo is .txt/.json/.py/.md plus .gitignore and LICENSE, and images/ is gitignored.
# `text=auto` detects binary content on its own, so adding one later is safe; add an explicit
# `*.ext binary` line anyway if you ever track images, to skip the detection entirely.
#
# Applied with `git add --renormalize .` as its own commit, verified with
# `git diff --cached --ignore-cr-at-eol` returning empty (i.e. ONLY endings changed).

* text=auto eol=lf